WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.667
Cedar Vale signs up for three years of
service.

00:02.727 --> 00:06.289
The price is agreed from the start: twelve
thousand dollars in

00:06.349 --> 00:09.912
year one, thirteen thousand two hundred in
year two, and fourteen

00:09.972 --> 00:12.639
thousand five hundred and twenty in year
three.

00:13.300 --> 00:15.135
How should HubSpot represent that?

00:15.195 --> 00:18.608
Start with one governing agreement and a
dated price schedule.

00:18.668 --> 00:22.316
Each anniversary moves to the next agreed
price; it is not

00:22.376 --> 00:25.747
automatically a new sale or a renewal
negotiation.

00:26.400 --> 00:30.197
In this fictional example, the commitment
runs from January twenty

00:30.257 --> 00:32.860
twenty-seven through December twenty
twenty-nine.

00:32.920 --> 00:35.245
Each price applies for exactly one year.

00:35.305 --> 00:39.209
The total across the three years is
thirty-nine thousand seven hundred

00:39.269 --> 00:40.485
and twenty dollars.

00:41.167 --> 00:44.089
The contract term tells us how long the
commitment lasts.

00:44.149 --> 00:46.965
The price schedule tells us which rate
applies when.

00:47.025 --> 00:51.057
Billing frequency answers a separate
question: how often do we issue

00:51.117 --> 00:52.077
an invoice?

00:52.733 --> 00:56.552
With annual billing, the example has one
charge of twelve thousand

00:56.612 --> 01:00.601
dollars in year one, then thirteen thousand
two hundred, then fourteen

01:00.661 --> 01:02.603
thousand five hundred and twenty.

01:02.663 --> 01:06.503
Invoice issue and due dates still follow the
agreed billing terms.

01:07.167 --> 01:10.964
With monthly billing, year one has twelve
charges of one thousand

01:11.024 --> 01:11.877
dollars.

01:11.937 --> 01:14.199
Year two has twelve of eleven hundred.

01:14.259 --> 01:16.648
Year three has twelve of twelve hundred and
ten.

01:16.708 --> 01:19.716
Both patterns cover the same three-year
price schedule.

01:20.367 --> 01:23.908
Keep the source agreement and its price
schedule identifiable.

01:23.968 --> 01:27.467
The commercial Contract represents the
commitment being managed.

01:27.527 --> 01:31.111
The Deal records the sales opportunity, and
a Quote presents the

01:31.171 --> 01:32.984
proposal when that route is used.

01:33.667 --> 01:37.677
Represent each period with its own start,
finite term, quantity and

01:37.737 --> 01:38.932
applicable price.

01:38.992 --> 01:40.869
The three phases are sequential.

01:40.929 --> 01:45.132
If all three prices start together, you have
described concurrent charges

01:45.192 --> 01:46.963
instead of a yearly increase.

01:47.633 --> 01:50.855
Keep the full commitment separate from the
current period's rate.

01:50.915 --> 01:54.712
Thirty-nine thousand seven hundred and
twenty dollars is the three-year

01:54.772 --> 01:55.497
total.

01:55.557 --> 01:58.779
It is not this year's recurring rate or
evidence of cash

01:58.839 --> 01:59.628
collected.

02:00.300 --> 02:04.567
HubSpot now documents ramp pricing in
Revenue Hub quotes: add the

02:04.627 --> 02:08.701
product as separate line items, set each
phase's billing start date,

02:08.761 --> 02:10.980
and give each phase its intended term.

02:11.040 --> 02:15.157
This is a documented option, not a
completed setup demonstration.

02:15.833 --> 02:19.844
That quote route requires Revenue Hub
Professional or Enterprise and a

02:19.904 --> 02:21.099
Revenue Hub seat.

02:21.159 --> 02:25.233
The documentation also states a restriction
on ramp pricing with

02:25.293 --> 02:26.573
HubSpot payments.

02:26.633 --> 02:30.964
Verify the processor and account path
before promising automated billing.

02:31.633 --> 02:35.068
Direct Contracts offer dated line items
through a beta route.

02:35.128 --> 02:39.224
Billing migration also documents ramp
phases, but it is a separate

02:39.284 --> 02:40.201
process.

02:40.261 --> 02:43.675
A migration ramp key is not a field every
quote needs.

02:43.735 --> 02:46.657
Do not infer that all these routes behave
identically.

02:47.333 --> 02:51.536
If Finance bills elsewhere, keep that system
responsible for its invoice

02:51.596 --> 02:53.281
schedule and check the handoff.

02:53.341 --> 02:57.544
Recording three prices in HubSpot alone
does not configure the external

02:57.604 --> 02:58.649
billing system.

02:59.300 --> 03:01.753
Now test the boundary, not just the total.

03:01.813 --> 03:05.931
On December thirty-first, twenty
twenty-seven, the example is still in

03:05.991 --> 03:06.823
year one.

03:06.883 --> 03:10.808
From January first, twenty twenty-eight, year
two's price applies.

03:10.868 --> 03:13.961
There should be no overlap and no missing
service period.

03:14.633 --> 03:18.601
For the monthly pattern, the expected rate
changes from one thousand

03:18.661 --> 03:20.219
to eleven hundred dollars.

03:20.279 --> 03:23.073
The old phase must stop before the new one
charges.

03:23.133 --> 03:26.803
Adding both would produce twenty-one
hundred dollars for the same month.

03:27.467 --> 03:30.837
Repeat the check at the next anniversary,
when the monthly rate

03:30.897 --> 03:32.924
becomes twelve hundred and ten dollars.

03:32.984 --> 03:36.781
For annual billing, check the corresponding
annual amount instead.

03:36.841 --> 03:42.217
These are expected results; no invoices or
automatic transitions were executed

03:42.277 --> 03:42.939
here.

03:43.600 --> 03:46.928
At the end of year three, confirm what the
agreement actually

03:46.988 --> 03:47.735
says.

03:47.795 --> 03:51.507
Do not invent a fourth-year price or assume
automatic renewal.

03:51.567 --> 03:55.407
Keep the source terms, commercial records
and billing schedule aligned.

03:56.067 --> 03:57.944
Reconcile the complete schedule.

03:58.004 --> 04:01.567
Twelve thousand, plus thirteen thousand two
hundred, plus fourteen

04:01.627 --> 04:05.723
thousand five hundred and twenty equals
thirty-nine thousand seven

04:05.783 --> 04:07.425
hundred and twenty dollars.

04:07.485 --> 04:11.965
Thirty-six monthly charges produce the
same total before taxes and other

04:12.025 --> 04:13.241
agreed adjustments.

04:13.900 --> 04:17.633
Use the configuration checklist to choose a
route: which quote or

04:17.693 --> 04:22.109
Contract experience is available, which
system controls billing, and what

04:22.169 --> 04:24.004
evidence proves each transition?

04:24.064 --> 04:28.523
Treat report values and revenue recognition
as separate questions with

04:28.583 --> 04:29.927
their own definitions.

04:30.600 --> 04:33.715
The companion pack gives you the
phased-pricing worksheet,

04:33.775 --> 04:37.252
configuration decision checklist and
transition tests.

04:37.312 --> 04:40.917
Map one real agreement, name the owner,
compare expected and

04:40.977 --> 04:44.817
observed results, and resolve discrepancies
before automating.
